-- title:
--   The inner loop of one round of Algorithm 3 as a path law; $\mathcal S^j$ and $J$ on a path
-- statement:
--   One round of Algorithm 3 runs the while loop of Lines 5–10 from the initial state. Its **path law** is the distribution of the sequence of states $(\mathcal S^0,\mathcal F^1),(\mathcal S^1,\mathcal F^2),\dots,(\mathcal S^L,\mathcal F^{L+1})$ obtained by iterating the step $L$ times, where $L\ge2\bar R\log_2|\mathcal F|+N$.
--
--   On a path, $\mathcal S^j$ is the multiset at index $j$ (the last one for $j$ beyond the path, matching $\mathcal S^j:=\mathcal S^J$ for $j>J$), and
--   $$
--   J=\min\{j : \text{every element of }\mathcal S^j\text{ has length }n\},
--   $$
--   "the total number of iterations executed by Algorithm 3" (p.26).
--
--   **Formalization Note** The step is the identity on terminal states, so by Lemma 3.9 this is the paper's loop. The filtration $(\mathscr F^j)$ of p.26 is generated by the path prefix. This path is the first round (cache: $\hat\mu(\emptyset)$ and the reads of Line 4); the particles and classes do not depend on the cache, so every round has the same law of particles.
-- source:
--   Golowich, Moitra, Rohatgi, The Power of Test-Time Training for Approximate Sampling, arXiv:2606.11437 (version of November 3, 2026), Algorithm 3, p.12 (PDF p.14); §3.2, p.26 (PDF p.28)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_TTTSampling_alg3Step

open Classical

namespace TTTSampling

/-- `k` iterations of a Markov kernel from `s`, as the law of the path `[s₀, s₁, …, s_k]`. -/
noncomputable def chain {β : Type} (step : β → PMF β) : ℕ → β → PMF (List β)
  | 0, s => PMF.pure [s]
  | k + 1, s => (step s).bind fun s' => (chain step k s').map (fun p => s :: p)

/-- **The inner loop of one round of Algorithm 3** (Lines 3–10), as the law of the path of states
`(𝒮^0, ℱ^1, Q), …, (𝒮^L, ℱ^{L+1}, Q)` with `L = pathLen` (see `pathLen`), started from the initial
state of the first round (cache: the convention value `µ̂(∅)` and the reads of Line 4).

Golowich, Moitra, Rohatgi, arXiv:2606.11437 (version of November 3, 2026), Algorithm 3, printed
p.12 (PDF p.14), and §3.2, p.26 (PDF p.28): "for all j > J we set 𝒮^j := 𝒮^J".

**Formalization Note** The while loop is the Markov kernel `alg3Step`, which is the identity on
terminal states, run for `L ≥ 2R̄ log₂ |ℱ| + N` iterations; by Lemma 3.9 this is the paper's loop.
The randomness of the round is that of the successive calls of Algorithm 2, so the σ-algebra
`ℱ^j` of p.26 (all random variables up to iteration `j`) is generated by the path prefix. -/
noncomputable def alg3Path {α : Type} [Fintype α] [LinearOrder α] {n : ℕ} (C_N : ℕ) (R δ : ℝ)
    (F : Finset (PMF (Fin n → α))) (μh : List α → NNReal) : PMF (List (A3State α n)) :=
  chain (alg3Step R μh) (pathLen C_N F.card R δ) (alg3Init C_N R δ F μh {[]})

/-- The state at index `j` of a path (the last state for `j` beyond the end, matching
`𝒮^j := 𝒮^J` for `j > J`). -/
def stAt {α : Type} {n : ℕ} (p : List (A3State α n)) (j : ℕ) : A3State α n :=
  p.getD (min j (p.length - 1)) ⟨0, ∅, ∅⟩

/-- The multiset `𝒮^j` of a path. -/
def sAt {α : Type} {n : ℕ} (p : List (A3State α n)) (j : ℕ) : Multiset (List α) :=
  (stAt p j).S

/-- `J`, "the smallest index such that every element of 𝒮^J has length n" (p.26), on a path: the
first index of a terminal state (the path length if there is none). -/
noncomputable def stopIdx {α : Type} {n : ℕ} (p : List (A3State α n)) : ℕ :=
  p.findIdx (fun s => decide s.Terminal)

end TTTSampling


